
#  Spring IoC Container (Inversion of Control)

   Spring IoC container is the core of spring framework.
   It creates objects, configures them and manages their entire life cycle.
   The container utilizes Dependency Injection (DI) for managing components and their dependencies.

   Information about objects, knows as Beans, is obtained from configuration files (XML),Java code or Java Annotations.
   IoC is achieved as developers do not control the lifecycle of java objects; instead, the Container manages it.

## Without IoC

   Without inversion of control, you are in charge of the creation of objects.
   In an inversion of control scenario a framework is in charge to create instances of a class.

   In the traditional approach, obtaining an address instance for an Employee would involve
   creating a new instance of the Address class.
   However, when using Spring, the framework takes care of creating all dependent objects for us,
   eliminating the need for manual object instantiation.

### How to achieve IoC

    Dependency Injection is the method through which IoC can be achieved.

#### Benefits of IoC

    - All the classes and their lifecycle will be managed by Spring from creating to destruction.
    - Loose coupling between classes.
    - Better testability, By using interfaces in the constructor, we can effortlessly
      substitute the implementation with a mock implementation